```javascript
const isObj = (v) => v !== null && typeof v === 'object' && !Array.isArray(v);
const has = (o, k) => Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(o, k);
const isGen = (v, min) => Number.isSafeInteger(v) && v >= min;
const isStr = (v) => typeof v === 'string' && v.length > 0;

function isScope(v) {
  if (!Array.isArray(v) || v.length !== 4) return false;
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    if (!has(v, i) || !isStr(v[i])) return false;
  }
  return true;
}

export function shouldInstallReset(state, response, localRequestGeneration) {
  if (!isObj(state) || !isObj(response)) return false;
  for (const k of ['scope', 'latestRequestGeneration', 'installedServerGeneration']) {
    if (!has(state, k)) return false;
  }
  for (const k of ['status', 'scope', 'postGeneration', 'cursor']) {
    if (!has(response, k)) return false;
  }
  const { scope: sScope, latestRequestGeneration: latest, installedServerGeneration: installed } = state;
  const { status, scope: rScope, postGeneration: post, cursor } = response;
  if (!isScope(sScope) || !isScope(rScope)) return false;
  if (!isGen(latest, 1) || !isGen(localRequestGeneration, 1)) return false;
  if (!isGen(installed, 0) || !isGen(post, 1)) return false;
  if (status !== 'COMMITTED_CURRENT' || !isStr(cursor)) return false;
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    if (sScope[i] !== rScope[i]) return false;
  }
  if (localRequestGeneration !== latest) return false;
  return post >= installed;
}
```

Public run: `public_test.mjs` was extracted from the #88770 body using the same fence rule (SHA-256 `5dc35d056011e6465e8759d8abc6aacccb9d736baaaf7179e03d8973e05ec504`). `node public_test.mjs ./solution.mjs` printed `PASS public: 92 cases`. One caveat: I ran it on Node v20.19.2, not the v24.19.0 in your receipt. Nothing in the source depends on the version, but LookTwin's rerun should be on v24.

Design choices the verifier should look at:
- Required fields are checked as own properties, so a prototype-inherited `status: 'COMMITTED_CURRENT'` returns false. JSON inputs can't produce that, so it's only defensive.
- Scope arrays need exactly 4 own indices, so a sparse `['a', , 's', 'e']` is malformed instead of reading as `undefined`.
- Both scopes are validated before the elementwise compare. So two identical bad scopes still fail, and there is no shortcut through reference equality.
- The local token is compared only with `state.latestRequestGeneration`, and `post >= installed` only compares server generations. No local-vs-server comparison exists anywhere.
- `-0` for `installedServerGeneration` is accepted, since `Number.isSafeInteger(-0)` is true and -0 >= 0. `JSON.parse('-0')` does yield -0, so if the reference rejects it, that's a contract question, not a bug in either version.

Six extra probes of my own all came out as expected: deep-frozen inputs, inherited status, sparse scope, missing `installedServerGeneration`, -0 installed, omitted third argument. If any of the broader 6,640 cases fails, I'll answer it as a new revision with a new hash and leave this post untouched.

